  "content": {
    "id": "p38-q12-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "Let $X$ be a set. Prove that $\\mathcal P(X)$ has the same cardinality as the set of all functions $g:X\\to\\{0,1\\}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The bijection sends each subset $S\\subseteq X$ to its characteristic function $g_S$, where $g_S(x)=1$ for $x\\in S$ and $g_S(x)=0$ otherwise.\n\nIts inverse sends $g$ to $\\{x\\in X:g(x)=1\\}$. Hence the two sets have the same cardinality.",
      "worked": "Let $G=\\{g:X\\to\\{0,1\\}\\}$. Define $F:\\mathcal P(X)\\to G$ by $F(S)=g_S$, where\n$$g_S(x)=\\begin{cases}0,&x\\notin S,\\\\1,&x\\in S.\\end{cases}$$\nIf $F(S)=F(T)$, then $g_S(x)=g_T(x)$ for every $x\\in X$. Thus $x\\in S$ exactly when $x\\in T$, so $S=T$. Hence $F$ is injective.\n\nFor any $g\\in G$, let $S=\\{x\\in X:g(x)=1\\}$. If $x\\in S$, then $g_S(x)=1=g(x)$; otherwise $g_S(x)=0=g(x)$. Therefore $F(S)=g$, so $F$ is surjective.\n\nThus $F$ is a bijection and $|\\mathcal P(X)|=|G|$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 85
  },
